# Criss-cross whose two latest common ancestors are at different distances
# from the current head. The closer one is the second parent of the given
# branch's merge. Searching from the given branch hits the other ancestor
# first and would delete f.txt. The latest ancestor conflicts instead.
I definitions.inc
> init
<<<
> branch given
<<<
> checkout given
<<<
+ f.txt wug.txt
> add f.txt
<<<
> commit "Add f.txt containing wug.txt"
<<<
> branch B
<<<
> checkout master
<<<
+ f.txt notwug.txt
> add f.txt
<<<
> commit "Add f.txt containing notwug.txt"
<<<
> checkout given
<<<
> merge master
Encountered a merge conflict.
<<<
= f.txt conflict_b1.txt
+ f.txt notwug.txt
> add f.txt
<<<
> commit "Reset f to notwug.txt"
<<<
> rm f.txt
<<<
> commit "given now empty."
<<<
> checkout B
<<<
+ g.txt wug2.txt
> add g.txt
<<<
> commit "Added g.txt"
<<<
> checkout master
<<<
> merge B
Encountered a merge conflict.
<<<
= f.txt conflict_b2.txt
+ f.txt wug.txt
> add f.txt
<<<
> commit "Reset f to wug.txt"
<<<
> merge given
Encountered a merge conflict.
<<<
= f.txt conflict_b3.txt
= g.txt wug2.txt
